Our 7-feature ensemble outperforms all individual baselines by 6--11\,pp in 3-class F1; multi-detector ensembles achieve moderately higher 3-class F1 (0.785--0.795 vs.\ 0.774) with comparable binary F1 ($\leq$0.3\,pp gap) at 2.4$\times$ the computational cost.


\subsection{Downstream Utility}
\label{sec:results-downstream}

If depth fingerprinting reliably identifies recursive text, can it improve data curation?
Fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on five data variants (3 seeds each) shows that at equal sample count ($n$\,=\,3{,}951), depth-aware selection is practically equivalent to random subsampling (TOST $\pm$2\% margin, $p = 1.1 \times 10^{-4}$), but data quantity dominates: the full dataset ($n$\,=\,12{,}299) yields significantly lower perplexity (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).
Depth profiling serves a diagnostic rather than prescriptive role: the practical value lies in corpus-level auditing, not sample-level filtering.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced generation depth classification and documented differential depth response across eight generators from five families: a 7-feature ensemble exploiting the order-of-magnitude gap between drifting and near-stationary features achieves binary F1\,=\,0.764 on cross-model chains (0.694 LOGO), while equivalence testing confirms that the detected shift does not degrade downstream training utility (TOST $\pm$2\%, $p < 0.001$).
Boundaries remain at instruction tuning and frontier scale (70B+), where compressed feature margins may limit detectability; code, data, and models will be released upon publication.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig2_framework.pdf}
\caption{Overview of the depth fingerprinting framework. Given a text, reference-model features (entropy statistics under Pythia-410M) and surface features (lexical diversity) are extracted and fed to an XGBoost classifier trained under Leave-One-Generator-Out cross-validation. The feature set combines drifting indicators (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) that capture the monotonic location shift with stationary indicators (entropy max, tail decay) that provide a stable baseline, exploiting their differential depth response for generation depth classification.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Task Formulation}
\label{sec:task}

Given a text $x$, the goal is to predict its \emph{generation depth} $d \in \{0, 1, 2{+}\}$, where $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text, $d{=}1$ denotes first-generation LLM output conditioned on human text, and $d{=}2{+}$ denotes text produced by recursively conditioning an LLM on its own prior output at least twice.
Formally, let $\mathcal{C}_0$ be a reference corpus of human-written text.
A generation chain of depth $k$ is defined recursively: $x^{(0)} \sim \mathcal{C}_0$, and $x^{(k)} = G(x^{(k-1)})$ for $k \geq 1$, where $G$ denotes stochastic LLM generation conditioned on its input.
We frame this as a coarse ordinal classification task over three classes rather than binary real-vs-synthetic detection. This enables finer-grained provenance characterization.
Depths beyond 2 are merged into a single $2{+}$ class, consistent with monotonic feature convergence that compresses inter-class margins beyond depth 2 (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}).

\subsection{Generation Chain Construction}
\label{sec:generation}

We construct generation chains using eight generators spanning five model families: \textbf{Pythia} (2.8B, 1B) \cite{pythia}, \textbf{OLMo} (OLMo-2-1B) \cite{olmo}, \textbf{Qwen} (Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-8B-Base) \cite{qwen3}, \textbf{Llama} (Llama-3.2-3B) \cite{llama3}, and \textbf{Mistral} (Mistral-7B-v0.3) \cite{mistral7b}.
This selection covers parameter scales from 0.6B to 8B and diverse pretraining corpora, which allows evaluation of cross-generator and cross-scale generalization.

For each generator, we sample approximately 1{,}000 seed passages from English Wikipedia as $\mathcal{C}_0$.
The same pool of seed passages serves as gen-0 for all generators.
We use shared seeds across generators to ensure that cross-generator comparisons control for input content, isolating the effect of the generation model from source text variation.
In LOGO evaluation, gen-0 samples from the held-out generator's chains also appear (with the same text) in the training generators' chains, creating a potential train--test overlap for the gen-0 class.
We address this with a supplementary binary evaluation excluding gen-0 entirely (\S\ref{sec:results-baselines}).
Each seed is iteratively fed through the generator to produce a chain of depth 4 (i.e., gen-0 through gen-4), using the prompt ``\texttt{Continue the following text: \{text\}}''.
All generation uses temperature $\tau{=}0.7$, nucleus sampling with $p{=}0.9$, and maximum length of 256 new tokens.
The theoretical yield is $8 \times {\sim}1{,}000 \times 5 \approx 40{,}000$ texts; after removing generation failures, exact duplicates, and passages shorter than 50 tokens, 37{,}058 texts remain (Appendix~\ref{app:dataset-stats}).
For classification, depths 2--4 are collapsed into the $2{+}$ class.

\subsection{Feature Extraction}
\label{sec:features}

The seven features are motivated by two complementary views of distributional drift.
Reference-model features capture how the text's token-level surprise distribution shifts relative to a fixed model; surface features capture how lexical diversity contracts under recursive generation.
Together, they span both the generative mechanism (what the model produces) and the surface manifestation (how the vocabulary changes); Figure~\ref{fig:framework} provides an overview.
This two-pronged design is not post-hoc: it follows directly from the observation that recursive generation exhibits a location shift in the NLL distribution (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}).
The seven features were selected a priori; no larger candidate set was explored.

We extract seven distributional features per text, organized into two groups based on what they measure rather than how they are computed.

\paragraph{Reference-model features.}
A held-out reference model (Pythia-410M, chosen to be smaller than all generators) computes per-token negative log-likelihood (NLL) for each text.
Although Pythia-410M shares the GPT-NeoX architecture with two of the eight generators, cross-family reference model experiments (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) confirm that depth features are not reference-specific (LOGO F1 varies by $\leq$0.013 across Pythia, OLMo, and Qwen references).
From the resulting NLL sequence, we extract five statistics:\footnote{We use ``entropy'' as shorthand for token-level negative log-likelihood under the reference model, following convention in prior detection work. These are point-wise NLL values, not Shannon entropy of the full distribution.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{entropy\_mean}: mean token-level NLL, capturing the average predictability of the text under the reference model;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_std}: standard deviation of token-level NLL, measuring the spread of per-token surprise;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_max}: maximum token-level NLL, capturing the most surprising token;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_min}: minimum token-level NLL, capturing the most predictable token. As generation depth increases, the floor of token-level surprise drops, reflecting the model's increasing confidence on its own outputs.
    Although entropy\_min shows near-zero marginal impact in leave-one-feature-out analysis ($\Delta$F1\,=\,+0.01\,pp, within bootstrap noise), we retain it as the minimum token-level NLL provides a natural distributional anchor for the reference model's confidence floor;
    \item \textbf{tail\_decay}: slope of the vocabulary-level averaged log-probability distribution in the mid-rank region (ranks 100--1000).
    For each text, we compute the mean log-probability of each vocabulary token across all positions under the reference model, sort tokens in descending order of mean log-probability, and fit a linear regression to ranks 100--1000.
    The slope captures how steeply the reference model's probability mass decays beyond high-probability tokens, characterizing the shape of the distribution tail.
    Ranks 100--1000 capture the transition from head to tail of the frequency distribution, below function words and above hapax legomena.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Surface features.}
Two features capture lexical diversity without requiring a reference model:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{TTR}: type-token ratio (number of unique word types divided by total tokens);
    \item \textbf{hapax\_ratio}: proportion of words appearing exactly once (\emph{hapax legomena}).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The grouping reflects an empirical pattern we term \emph{differential depth response}: central-tendency and diversity features (entropy\_mean, TTR, hapax\_ratio) drift substantially ($|\rho| = 0.60$--$0.63$, $d = 0.76$--$1.17$); entropy\_std shows a moderate level shift ($d = 0.55$) despite low monotonicity ($|\rho| = 0.17$); entropy\_min exhibits change concentrated at the gen-0/gen-1 boundary ($|\rho| = 0.40$) but is effectively stationary for gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+ ($d = 0.03$); and shape features (entropy\_max, tail\_decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$, $d < 0.10$).
We adopt $|\rho| < 0.10$ as a practical threshold for near-zero monotonic association (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}).
This differential behavior, consistent with recursive generation shifting the location of the NLL distribution while shape features show an order-of-magnitude smaller effect, motivates combining both feature types.
As shown in \S\ref{sec:results}, the full 7-feature set significantly outperforms either group alone.
TTR and hapax ratio are strongly correlated ($r = 0.97$), reducing effective dimensionality to ${\sim}$5; both are retained because LOFO confirms positive contribution from each and the DDR partition is defined by depth-sensitivity ($\rho$, $d$), not feature independence.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

Three overlapping generator subsets are used: the original 5-generator set (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen) for cross-model chains, a 6-generator set (adding Qwen3-8B) for appendix analyses, and the full 8-generator set (adding Llama-3.2-3B and Mistral-7B-v0.3) for single-generator LOGO; each table notes its subset.

\paragraph{Cross-validation.}
To evaluate generalization to unseen generators, we use \textbf{Leave-One-Generator-Out (LOGO)} cross-validation: in each fold, one of the eight generators is held out for testing and the remaining seven provide training data.
We additionally report \textbf{Leave-One-Family-Out (LOFO)} results, where an entire model family (e.g., all Qwen models) is held out, testing generalization across architectural lineages.

\paragraph{Classifier.}
We use XGBoost \cite{xgboost} throughout ($n\_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max\_\text{depth}{=}6$, learning rate $0.1$), with identical hyperparameters across all methods in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}.
LOGO F1 is stable across random seeds (std\,$<$\,0.001 over 5 seeds).
Alternative classifiers and ordinal-specific methods (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, Frank-Hall \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}) yield comparable performance (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}); discriminative power resides in the features, not the classifier.

\paragraph{Metrics and statistical testing.}
The primary metric is \textbf{macro-averaged F1} over the three depth classes.
We report 95\% confidence intervals via percentile bootstrap ($n{=}1{,}000$), McNemar tests for pairwise comparisons, and paired bootstrap for baseline comparisons.
All $p$-values are two-sided; Bonferroni correction is applied where multiple comparisons arise.
Feature--depth correlations (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) use class-balanced subsamples to control for depth-class imbalance.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare the 7-feature ensemble against four single-score detection methods repurposed as depth predictors: Avg-LogProb, FastDetectGPT \cite{fast_detectgpt}, Binoculars \cite{binoculars}, and LD-Score \cite{ld_score}.
Each baseline score is used as a univariate feature in the same XGBoost framework under LOGO cross-validation, ensuring a controlled comparison.

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Since TTR and hapax\_ratio are mechanically length-dependent, we verify depth signal is not a length artifact via length-matched subsampling that equalizes length distributions across depth classes (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}; Appendix~\ref{app:length}).

